<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- NuGet packaging metadata -->
    <Description>Dependency-free cryptographic primitives for the SharedKernel: secret-agnostic one-way hashing (PBKDF2-HMACSHA256, suitable for passwords, API keys, recovery codes, and other one-way secrets), AES-256-GCM symmetric encryption (sync and async, plus an additive envelope-encryption seam and a bounded-TTL caching decorator for the key provider), RSA/ECDSA asymmetric signing, HMACSHA256 signing, and cryptographically secure random/token generation. Pure BCL System.Security.Cryptography only — zero third-party NuGet dependencies. AOT-compatible. Decoupled from identity/OIDC concerns so non-web worker services can consume it directly. Depends on SharedKernel.Primitives and SharedKernel.Configuration. v2.0.0: IPasswordHasher renamed to IOneWayHasher (breaking) to remove auth-domain vocabulary from a 01.Core primitive. Shipped (P-446/WO-068): IEncryptionKeyProvider's GetCurrentKey()/GetKey(string) synchronous members were removed and replaced by GetCurrentKeyAsync/GetKeyAsync; ISymmetricEncryptionService gained additive EncryptAsync/DecryptAsync/EncryptToStringAsync/DecryptToStringAsync overloads. Shipped (P-491/WO-081, breaking): every ISymmetricEncryptionService member (Encrypt/EncryptAsync/Decrypt/DecryptAsync/EncryptToString(Async)/DecryptToString(Async)) gained a required byte[] associatedData parameter with no default value — every call site across every consuming domain must add an explicit argument (pass Array.Empty&lt;byte&gt;() when no natural context binding exists); a mismatched associatedData at decrypt time fails authentication exactly like a tampered ciphertext or wrong key. Shipped (P-492/WO-081, breaking BEHAVIOR change, not a compile-time API break — no signature changed): a new ISynchronousEncryptionKeyProvider marker interface and EncryptionKeyProviderCapabilities.IsGenuinelySynchronous(IEncryptionKeyProvider) capability check replace the retained sync Encrypt/Decrypt/EncryptToString/DecryptToString members' unconditional GetAwaiter().GetResult() bridge — computed once at AesGcmEncryptionService construction time. Against a provider not confirmed genuinely synchronous (directly, or recursively through CachedEncryptionKeyProvider.Inner) — notably any KMS/HSM-backed IEncryptionKeyProvider, which must never implement the marker — those four members now throw NotSupportedException immediately, directing the caller to the corresponding *Async overload, instead of silently blocking a real thread on a network/IPC round trip as they previously did. Any existing custom IEncryptionKeyProvider implementer that relied on that silent blocking now observes a behavior change at the same call sites with no source change of their own; a genuinely synchronous provider (the config-backed default) is byte-for-byte unaffected once it additionally implements ISynchronousEncryptionKeyProvider (see README's "Symmetric Encryption" section for the full migration guidance and usage examples). Shipped (P-493/WO-081, breaking): IAsymmetricKeyProvider's GetRsaKey(string)/GetEcdsaKey(string) synchronous members were removed outright and replaced by GetRsaKeyAsync/GetEcdsaKeyAsync; IAsymmetricSignatureService gained additive SignAsync/VerifyAsync overloads, with the retained sync Sign/Verify now gated by a new ISynchronousAsymmetricKeyProvider marker and AsymmetricKeyProviderCapabilities.IsGenuinelySynchronous(IAsymmetricKeyProvider) capability check (a direct marker check only, mirroring P-492's gate but with no decorator-unwrapping, since no caching decorator exists yet for IAsymmetricKeyProvider) — against an unmarked provider those two members now throw NotSupportedException immediately instead of silently blocking a real thread. RsaSignatureService/EcdsaSignatureService no longer dispose the RSA/ECDsa instance an IAsymmetricKeyProvider hands back — that instance is not caller-owned, a fix for a latent ObjectDisposedException hazard that was unreachable until now because no IAsymmetricKeyProvider implementation shipped anywhere in the platform. Verify now applies the same minimum-key-size check Sign already applied (RSA: 2048 bits; ECDSA gained a 256-bit minimum check on both members, having previously had none at all). A genuine BCL limitation is recorded rather than glossed over: RSA/ECDsa's SignData/VerifyData have no async overload anywhere in the BCL, so SignAsync/VerifyAsync only make key resolution non-blocking — the cryptographic call itself, against a remote-KMS-backed key, remains a real synchronous network operation on the calling thread (see README's "Asymmetric Signing" section for the full migration guidance and usage examples).</Description>
    <PackageReleaseNotes>WO-083 / P-514 (BREAKING): ITotpReplayGuard's two-step HasBeenUsedAsync(check)+MarkUsedAsync(mark) shape is REMOVED and replaced by one atomic member, TryMarkUsedAsync(identityKey, code, validityWindow, ct) returning true only when THIS call is the first to mark (identityKey, code) used and false when it was already marked (a replay) — closing a TOCTOU where two concurrent TotpVerifier.VerifyAsync calls presenting the same valid code could both observe "not yet used" and both be accepted. TotpVerifier.VerifyAsync gained new optional digits/stepSeconds/driftWindow/algorithm parameters (defaulting to 6/30/1/Sha1, the exact prior hardcoded values), inserted before the existing trailing ct parameter, and its replay window is now computed from those SAME actual values instead of two removed hardcoded DefaultStepSeconds/DefaultDriftWindow constants — a caller validating against a non-default step/drift configuration now gets a replay window that genuinely matches what it validated. A new standalone ITotpAttemptThrottle seam (IsThrottledAsync/RecordAttemptAsync) ships for RFC 4226 Section 7.3 attempt rate-limiting — consumer-implemented, never wired into TotpVerifier's constructor. MIGRATION: any existing ITotpReplayGuard implementer must collapse its HasBeenUsedAsync/MarkUsedAsync pair into one atomic TryMarkUsedAsync (e.g. ConcurrentDictionary.TryAdd, or a store's native atomic reservation primitive) — never a separate check followed by a separate set, which reopens the same TOCTOU. THIS IS NOT A ZERO-MIGRATION-COST CHANGE: two already-shipped implementers of the removed shape are known and require their own migration outside this package — 16.Testing/SharedKernel.Testing/Cryptography/FakeTotpReplayGuard.cs and 12.Security.Totp/SharedKernel.Security.Totp.Tests/Challenge/FakeTotpReplayGuard.cs. Additionally, and NOT anticipated by this phase's original design brief: 12.Security.Totp's PRODUCTION source, Challenge/TotpChallengeService.cs, calls TotpVerifier.VerifyAsync(identityKey, secret, code, ct) with ct passed POSITIONALLY as the 4th argument — since the new digits/stepSeconds/driftWindow/algorithm parameters are now inserted before ct, that positional 4th argument now binds to the new int digits parameter and fails to compile (CancellationToken is not implicitly convertible to int). That call site must be updated to pass ct by name (ct: ct) as part of 12.Security.Totp's own companion migration — it is a third real, previously-unflagged consumer of this breaking change, beyond the two already-known test fakes.
WO-083 / P-511 (fix, no signature changes): fixed a cross-caller cancellation-token leak in CachedEncryptionKeyProvider's single-flight refresh — one caller cancelling its own await could previously fault or cancel every other caller's concurrent, otherwise-healthy await of the same in-flight key resolution. The shared refresh is now driven by a cache-slot-owned CancellationTokenSource (never any individual caller's token); each caller awaits via Task.WaitAsync(callerCt) so only its own cancellation is ever observed by that caller. The underlying provider call remains genuinely cancellable once every caller has abandoned it (a per-slot waiter reference count reaching zero), and a slot that did not complete successfully by that point is evicted immediately so a future caller never joins an already-doomed resolution. Every other single-flight/fail-closed/no-stale-fallback guarantee (P-446) is unchanged.
WO-083 / P-512: added CryptographyOptions.MinimumPbkdf2Iterations (100,000) enforced via the existing [Range]/ValidateOnStart path — a configured Pbkdf2Iterations value below this floor now fails startup validation instead of silently accepting a value as low as 1. Pbkdf2OneWayHasher.Verify now rejects (HashVerificationResult.Failed) any stored hash whose embedded iteration count exceeds a new fixed MaxVerifiableIterations ceiling (2,000,000, independent of the configured value) BEFORE the expensive PBKDF2 derive call runs, closing a CPU-exhaustion vector from an attacker-supplied hash blob; also rejects a decoded subkey whose length is not exactly 32 bytes, and an iteration count &lt;= 0, both previously reaching Rfc2898DeriveBytes.Pbkdf2 uncontrolled or throwing an unhandled exception. The new floor applies only at Hash() time — every hash produced under a legitimate configuration below 100,000 prior to this release continues to verify exactly as before.
WO-083 / P-513: AesGcmEncryptionService now structurally rejects any CryptographicKey.Material whose length is not exactly 32 bytes (throwing CryptographicException, naming both the expected and actual length) before any AesGcm instance is constructed — closing a silent AES-128/AES-192 downgrade gap where a 16- or 24-byte key was previously accepted without complaint despite every doc on this package promising AES-256.</PackageReleaseNotes>
    <PackageTags>shared-kernel;cryptography;one-way-hashing;password-hashing;aes-gcm;encryption;envelope-encryption;signing;rsa;ecdsa;hmac;secure-random;aot</PackageTags>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\SharedKernel.Primitives\SharedKernel.Primitives.csproj" />
